<p>Every automatic path that exists to close it failed on the same call &mdash; the IB gateway
entered a partial wedge at about 19:57Z and the rider's own hard-flat, the watchdog, the EOD
flatten and the reconciler all share one <code>connectAsync</code>. The EOD flatten's timer is
<strong>Mon&ndash;Fri</strong>, so it does not retry over a weekend; its next elapse is Monday
20:53Z, and the tape reopens <strong>Sunday 22:00Z</strong>. That is a live-market window of about
23 hours with an unmanaged four-lot position in it.</p>
<p><strong>What is already done:</strong> <code>gazbot7-weekend-flatten.timer</code> is installed
and enabled, firing at the Sunday 22:00Z reopen and then every minute for two hours, self-disabling
once flat. <strong>What still needs you:</strong> confirm the gateway is answering before Sunday
22:00Z &mdash; nothing on this box can heal a wedge, and if it is still wedged at the reopen the
retry loop will fail sixty times in a row exactly as designed and the position will still be
there. That is SATURDAY&nbsp;#1.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="honest"><span class="n">★</span> THE HONEST HEADLINE &mdash; a small red week, and the
loss is not where you would look for it</h2>

<p class="lead">The desk booked <strong>&minus;$247.50</strong> across 43 clean lots,
<strong>3 of the five days green</strong>. Three sessions made $1,120.50
between them and two lost &minus;$1,368.00, and essentially
all of that loss is <strong>one day-rider position on Thursday</strong> that ran for six hours with
no stop in the water. The tournament &mdash; the eight base gates filling six live slots that most
of this report is about &mdash; traded only 36 lots all week for &minus;$58.50, because four of its
six slots were stood down and never fired at all.</p>

<p class="lead"><strong>The three things worth carrying into Saturday.</strong>
<em>One:</em> the standdown was right. Firing all six gates mechanically through this week's own
tape loses <strong>&minus;$1,905.36</strong> on 117 fires (Movement&nbsp;2), so the four silent
slots are the best-performing thing on the desk.
<em>Two:</em> your hands made $550.00 on 16 claims and the machine's own profit exits made
almost nothing &mdash; but the study that tried to automate your hand comes back
<strong>&minus;$4 out-of-sample</strong>, so the answer is not to copy it.
<em>Three:</em> the week's real damage was done by things that are not strategies at all &mdash; a
session-roll bug, an off-tape fill, a missing stop and a wedged gateway.</p>

