<div class="callout"><div class="ct">The three things this week actually taught the desk</div>
<p><strong>1 &middot; The trades table cannot tell you what you are carrying.</strong> It
enumerates what CLOSED, so it can never contradict a benign story. This week the router spent 137
consecutive ticks reciting closed rows and the word &ldquo;flat&rdquo; while
<code>updatePortfolio</code> in the same journal read <code>position=4.0</code>. Attribution has to
terminate at the venue position line, and &ldquo;not mine to close&rdquo; obliges escalation, not
dismissal.</p>
<p><strong>2 &middot; A filter that cannot fire is not a filter, it is an off-switch.</strong>
grind's 22-point ATR floor blocked 24,110 of 24,121 raw signals this week and sits at the p90 of
one-minute ATR. Every grind result in this report &mdash; the un-runnable trend-day rule, the two
empty exit-shadow watches, the zero live fires since 5 August &mdash; is downstream of that one
number, and nobody has ever swept it.</p>
<p><strong>3 &middot; Where you measure decides what you find.</strong> Three separate results
this week flipped on the measurement window rather than on the data: the shadow board reorders
completely once the untradeable Asia hours are voided; the idle-gate lab only says something
because a whole-week base rate was fired alongside the run windows; and the claim detector makes
+$1,069 in-sample and &minus;$4 out of it. <strong>Ask what the denominator is, every time.</strong></p></div>

