<li><strong>19:58:20Z</strong> &mdash; the IB gateway wedged. Last good venue read <strong>19:56:37Z</strong>
(&ldquo;position 4.0 managed&rdquo;); every tick since is <code>TimeoutError</code>, the watchdog has logged
<strong>73 consecutive blind cycles</strong> as of 22:20Z <em>and is still counting</em>, and there are
<strong>48 CLOSE-WAIT sockets on :4002</strong> &mdash; the documented wedge fingerprint. <strong>The 20:40 hard flat never executed.</strong></li>
</ol>

<div class="callout"><div class="ct"><span class="tag dr-open">UNRESOLVED</span> 4 LOTS LONG, NOT CONFIRMED FLAT</div>
<p>As of <strong>22:20Z</strong> the rider's book claims <strong>4 lots long from 29,491.625</strong> and
<code>desk_reconcile</code> reads <code>venue ? = tournament +0 + rider +4 &rarr; unaccounted ?</code>. The
position marks <strong>&minus;$887.00</strong> at the 20:40 price it should have been flattened at and
<strong>&minus;$973.00</strong> at the last print before the 21:00 halt. <strong>Neither number is a result and
I am not booking one.</strong> The standing rule is NEVER HOLD OVERNIGHT; the book is currently in breach of
it and the venue cannot be read to confirm either way. This is the first thing to check when the gateway
comes back, ahead of anything else in this report.</p></div>

<h4 class="dr">What the detector would have done on Friday</h4>

<p>Worth knowing, because a hand took the opposite side. Replaying the live detector on Friday's tape it
confirms at <strong>+9m, DOWN, at 29,340.50</strong>, on the strongest efficiency read of the week
(<strong>ER 0.854 / RT 0.553</strong>). That short would have gone <strong>+120.25 points (+$962)</strong> in
its favour and <strong>&minus;148.00 (&minus;$1,184)</strong> against, and held to the 20:40 clock it finishes
<strong>&minus;$326.00</strong> &mdash; the trail never arming. So the automatic rider had the direction right
and would still have lost money holding; it is not a clean &ldquo;the machine would have saved us&rdquo;. But
it is <strong>&minus;$326 instead of &minus;$973-and-counting</strong>, and it would have been flat before the
gateway wedged.</p>

<hr class="frag-sep">

<h3 id="dr-defect">6 &middot; ONE DEFECT FOUND WHILE DOING THIS</h3>

<div class="callout"><div class="ct">A MANUAL ENTRY OUTSIDE 13:30&ndash;21:00 SILENTLY DISABLES THE ATR TRAIL</div>
<p><code>do_manual_entry()</code> writes <code>arm_atr = drift_read(...).atr</code>. Outside 13:30&ndash;21:00
<code>drift.read()</code> returns early with <code>detail='before the 13:30 UTC open'</code> and
<strong><code>atr = 0.0</code></strong>. I confirmed that directly: both of Friday's manual entries stored
<code>arm_atr: 0.0</code>, and <code>trail_level()</code> therefore falls back to the fixed
<strong>ARM_PT = 150 pt</strong> instead of <strong>4&times;ATR</strong>. The live ticks say it in as many
words &mdash; <em>&ldquo;riding &middot; trail not armed (need +150 [fixed], at &minus;97)&rdquo;</em>.
At Friday's 30.7-point ATR, 4&times;ATR is <strong>122.8 pt</strong>, so the fallback is
<strong>27 points harder to arm</strong>. It cost <strong>$0 this week</strong> &mdash; neither position ever
went green &mdash; but on a hand-opened winner it silently removes the only mechanical exit the rider has.
Three independent confirmations: the code path, the state file, and the rider's own tick note.</p></div>

