span></td><td class="num">124</td><td class="num">&minus;$889.50</td><td class="num">&minus;$7.17</td><td class="num">34%</td><td class="num">42 / 43</td><td class="num">&minus;$1,672.00</td><td class="num">&minus;$1,593.50</td></tr><tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">require side*net30 &gt;= 1.0 x ATR <span class="tag">directional-net requirement</span></td><td class="num">117</td><td class="num">&minus;$675.00</td><td class="num">&minus;$5.77</td><td class="num">35%</td><td class="num">41 / 43</td><td class="num">&minus;$1,457.50</td><td class="num">&minus;$1,513.50</td></tr><tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">require side*net30 &gt;= 1.5 x ATR <span class="tag">directional-net requirement</span></td><td class="num">113</td><td class="num">&minus;$1,037.00</td><td class="num">&minus;$9.18</td><td class="num">33%</td><td class="num">37 / 43</td><td class="num">&minus;$1,819.50</td><td class="num">&minus;$1,513.50</td></tr><tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">require er30 &gt;= 0.25 <span class="tag">efficiency-ratio floor</span></td><td class="num">94</td><td class="num">&minus;$791.50</td><td class="num">&minus;$8.42</td><td class="num">35%</td><td class="num">33 / 43</td><td class="num">&minus;$1,574.00</td><td class="num">&minus;$1,403.00</td></tr><tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">require er30 &gt;= 0.35 <span class="tag">efficiency-ratio floor</span></td><td class="num">35</td><td class="num">&minus;$417.50</td><td class="num">&minus;$11.93</td><td class="num">31%</td><td class="num">11 / 43</td><td class="num">&minus;$1,092.50</td><td class="num">&minus;$1,043.00</td></tr><tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">require er30 &gt;= 0.45 <span class="tag">efficiency-ratio floor</span></td><td class="num">11</td><td class="num">&minus;$636.00</td><td class="num">&minus;$57.82</td><td class="num">18%</td><td class="num">2 / 43</td><td class="num">&minus;$804.50</td><td class="num">&minus;$833.50</td></tr><tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">clean-trend day AND side*net30&gt;=1.0xATR <span class="tag">the two stacked</span></td><td class="num">61</td><td class="num">$715.50</td><td class="num">$11.73</td><td class="num">39%</td><td class="num">24 / 43</td><td class="num">&minus;$67.00</td><td class="num">&minus;$123.00</td></tr><tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">clean-trend day AND er30&gt;=0.35 <span class="tag">the two stacked</span></td><td class="num">12</td><td class="num">$636.50</td><td class="num">$53.04</td><td class="num">50%</td><td class="num">6 / 43</td><td class="num">&minus;$38.50</td><td class="num">$11.00</td></tr></tbody></table>

<p class="ln">Read the last three columns, not the third. The best day-type rule
(<em>arm only on clean-trend days</em>) turns &minus;$958.00 into <strong>$580.50</strong>
&mdash; and then strip its best three fires and it is &minus;$202.00, leave one day out and
the worst fold is &minus;$123.00. It keeps 25 of the 43 winners,
so it is not a fake win; it is a real effect with no robustness underneath it. The stacked version
(<em>clean-trend day AND side*net30&gt;=1.0xATR</em>) is nominally the best cell in the whole table at
$715.50 on n=61, and it keeps only 24 of 43
winners. Neither is deployable.</p>

<p class="ln">Note also what the b- and c-family rows say, because they close a recurring
proposal: <strong>no directional-net requirement and no ER floor rescues grind</strong>. Every
single one of them stays negative. The ER-0.35 floor that is already live keeps 35 fires and still
loses &minus;$417.50. This desk has now refuted &ldquo;fix grind with an efficiency
filter&rdquo; on three separate occasions and it should stop being proposed.</p>

<div class="rev3"><div class="ct">★ AND THEN THE FINDING THAT ACTUALLY MATTERS — THE RULE CANNOT BE RUN</div><p>The day-type lab classified 240 sessions. <strong>97 of them are clean-trend days.</strong> The number that are clean-trend AND clear grind's 22-point ATR floor is <strong>1</strong>.</p><p>So &ldquo;arm grind on clean-trend days&rdquo; and &ldquo;grind's ATR floor lets it fire&rdquo; are very nearly mutually exclusive conditions. A clean trend is a day that goes somewhere smoothly; the 22-point floor wants a day that is violent. The rule that scores +$580 in the table above would, in production, have armed the gate on about one day in two hundred and forty — and on the other 239 the arming decision would have been irrelevant because the floor would have refused every signal anyway.</p><p><strong>This is why the mid-week lead is neither adopted nor refuted, but reframed.</strong> The question is not &lsquo;when should grind be armed?&rsquo;. Nothing about arming is binding. The question is whether 22 is the right floor, and that has never been tested — it is a number the gate was born with.</p></div>

<h3>2 &middot; So how reachable IS a 22-point ATR? Two independent measurements</h3>

<table><thead><tr><th class="ln">Measurement</th><th class="ln">Window</th><th class="num">Median 1-min ATR-14</th><th class="num">p90</th><th class="num">% of minutes &ge; 22pt</th><th class="num">Days with any such minute</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="ln">The p25 lab (via the parquet lake)</td><td class="ln">239 sessions</td><td class="num">14.66</td><td class="num">31.34</td><td class="num">25.3%</td><td class="num">213 of 239</td></tr><tr><td class="ln">This report, recomputed off <code>capture.db</code>'s own 5s bars</td><td class="ln">33 sessions, 2026-07-15 &rarr; 2026-08-21</td><td class="num">11.69</td><td class="num">22.64</td><td class="num">11.0%</td><td class="num">31 of 33</td></tr></tbody></table>

<p class="ln">The two disagree &mdash; 25% of minutes against 11% &mdash; and they are measuring
different windows, so both are printed rather than one being picked. What they agree on is the
shape, and the shape is the point: <strong>the 22-point floor sits at roughly the ninetieth
percentile of one-minute ATR</strong>. On the recent window it is almost exactly p90
(22.64). A floor at p90 is not a filter, it is an off-switch that opens during
violence, and it explains every grind number in this report: 24,110 signals blocked out of 24,121
this week, no live fire since 5 August, and two grind-exit shadow watches with nothing in them.</p>

<p class="ln">One more number worth having: <strong>31 of 33 days contain at
least one minute above the floor, but only 1 of 33 spend a quarter of the
session there.</strong> So &ldquo;the floor was reachable today&rdquo; is true almost every day and
means nothing; the floor is reachable for a few minutes of spike and then gone.</p>

