<p>Forty per cent of a hindsight ceiling, taken by the plainest exit on the board. (n=45 rather than 65 because the serial constraint drops runs that overlap an open position &mdash; that is the honest count a desk with one lot could actually have traded.) <strong>Read this next to the desk's own conversion: Movement 1 says we caught 4 runs and banked +$618 of a $1,038 ceiling, 60% &mdash; and FOUGHT a fifth for &minus;$1,288, so the desk's actual net near the week's runs was &minus;$670.</strong> So the exits on this desk are fine. The gap between $618 and $4,009 is not exit quality, it is <em>how many runs we were in</em>. Every remaining page of this movement is about that gap.</p>

<h3>2. Family A / Family B &mdash; which of the 65 were catchable at all?</h3>

<p>The mandate asks for a catchable/uncatchable split. Last Friday's VACUUM finding rules out doing it the obvious way: <strong>every pre-run price statistic on a census run list is an artefact of the census's own run-picker</strong>, which dedupes "keep-strongest" and therefore always lands on the local extremum of the move. Prior-5-minute price direction agrees with the run 48.66% of the time on unselected big moves and <strong>22.55%</strong> after the dedup &mdash; a 26-point shift created entirely by the selection rule. So a Family A/B split built on "what did the ten minutes before look like" would be measuring our own arithmetic.</p>

<p>The causal version instead asks: <strong>could a detector that reads only the move that has already happened get on board, in the right direction, while there was still money left?</strong> The detector is deliberately stupid &mdash; at the close of every minute, has the last ten minutes covered at least 2&times;ATR in one direction? Take that direction. That is the whole rule, and it cannot see the future.</p>

