<h4>6c. The volatility-shaped entry, and a look-ahead worth $86,000</h4>
<p>The brief's explicit question &mdash; <em>if direction is unpredictable but size is predictable, does a breakout-either-way entry beat a directional one?</em> &mdash; was tested properly, and the first pass was <strong>wrong</strong> in a way worth showing because nothing about the output revealed it.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Fill model on the identical straddle</th><th>n</th><th>Net</th><th>$/trade</th><th>Win %</th></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">TRIGGER-OPEN &mdash; <em>the bug</em>: filled at the open of the bar that went on to reach the level</td><td class="num">4,131</td><td class="num">+$53,216</td><td class="num">+$12.88</td><td class="num">65.8%</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">RESTING STOP &mdash; fill at the level + 1 tick + half a spread (what a real bracket does)</td><td class="num">4,131</td><td class="num">&minus;$32,710</td><td class="num">&minus;$7.92</td><td class="num">51.8%</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">NEXT OPEN &mdash; fill at the open of the bar after the trigger</td><td class="num">4,131</td><td class="num">&minus;$29,907</td><td class="num">&minus;$7.24</td><td class="num">51.6%</td></tr>
</table>

<p>An off-by-one in slice-offset arithmetic &mdash; <code>np.nonzero</code> returns the index inside the slice, so the triggering bar is <em>i+1+j</em> and it was stamped <em>i+j</em> &mdash; bought the bottom of the up-bar that triggered the buy. <strong>It is an $86,000 swing and the output looked perfect: plausible size, 65% wins, works both sides, stable across the year.</strong> Only re-reading the index arithmetic found it. With honest fills the best surviving cell (band 0.25&times;ATR, stop 5/trail 3) reads +$4.45 a trade over 4,135 <em>overlapping</em> positions &mdash; and overlapping is a lie, because up to eight run at once. One position at a time it is <strong>&minus;$8.96 a trade over 2,793</strong>. Re-raced on the 250ms quote tape where the fill order is <em>observed</em> rather than assumed, it is <strong>&minus;$18.24 a trade (n=445, 27 days)</strong>, and on the fresh five days <strong>&minus;$28.38 (n=89)</strong>. The mirror loses too. Dead three separate ways.</p>

<p>And the premise itself is false on gold: quintile the 20-minute range and the tightest quintile's next-hour range is 23.5bp against the widest quintile's 65.9bp. Compression expands <em>relatively</em> (1.29&times; vs 0.74&times;) but the absolute next-hour range still ranks with the prior one, so an ATR-sized straddle gets nothing. <strong>Compression does not predict expansion in a way you can trade.</strong></p>

