<p><strong>Look at the win-rate column and then ignore it, exactly as your judging rule says.</strong> Every candidate wins between 61% and 71% of its trades, including the one that loses $4,130. That is geometry, not quality: a 1.5-ATR stop against a 0.75-ATR target wins twice as often as it loses by construction and needs about 67% just to stand still. CT-8 wins 62% of 1,113 trades and is the worst gate in the table. This is the clearest illustration yet of why win% is off the kill list &mdash; and why it must never go on the <em>promotion</em> list either.</p>

<p><strong>CT-8 versus CT-7 is the L2 verdict in two rows.</strong> CT-7 is CT-8 plus the wall term. Adding the book takes 1,113 trades to 404 and &minus;$4,130 to &minus;$968 &mdash; but it did that by <em>removing trades, not by finding good ones</em>, and both numbers are still deep negative. A filter that improves a loser by cutting exposure is a volume cut wearing a filter's clothes. This desk has seen that exact shape before, in the rgv absorption-confirm.</p>

