<div class="card"><h3>The rule that would have been written, had the sign-flip control not killed it</h3>
<p><strong>ARM</strong> <code>vac_ign_brk</code> when <code>ATR(1m,14) &ge; 21.0pt</code> on MNQ <strong>and</strong> <code>13:00 &le; t &lt; 21:00Z</code>. <strong>BENCH</strong> below <code>19.0pt</code> (2-point hysteresis, matching the existing convention) with a <strong>&ge;5-minute dwell on the arm side</strong> &mdash; ATR crossing a bare threshold on a partial bar is exactly the oscillation that produced 20 switch changes for 4 trades on 08-07. <strong>No ER condition</strong>, for the reason below. Expectancy on that home regime only: <strong>+$18.03/trade over 95 trades</strong>. Blanket, as context and never as the verdict: +$3.21 over 284.</p>
<p><strong>Do not arm it.</strong> The home-regime number is the <em>same</em> number the fade control earns. Conditioning on ATR-high selects trades with more range in both directions, and a gate that makes money whichever way you point it is a volatility harvester with a stop, not an edge.</p>
</div>

<p><strong>The one thing here that is a genuine router finding, and it belongs to the router section rather than to any gate: the biggest runs start from chop.</strong> ER15 is the only feature measured whose AUC sits below 0.5, and it falls further as the runs get bigger &mdash; <strong>0.442 on the sat-out 65, 0.403 on the top-15</strong>. Every ER-floor arm condition the live router owns is standing gates down at the moment the largest moves begin. Two independent phases reached that conclusion this week and a third reached it last week from the filter side (ER cuts fail their placebo while ATR floors beat theirs).</p>

<p><strong>ER is also not a discriminator for the gates themselves, in either direction.</strong> Serially, VAC-IGN-BRK books &minus;$0.49/tr in dead chop (n=83), +$17.85/tr in the 0.15&ndash;0.35 middle (n=93) and &minus;$6.55/tr in clean trend (n=108). A middle bucket carrying the whole of a $911 net across three buckets is noise, not a regime. ⚠ Worth recording: the <em>unconstrained</em> run said +$22.28 in chop and +$13.79 in trend &mdash; that was the overlap-inflated ledger, exactly the number this movement warns against quoting.</p>

<h3>12. What could still be wrong with all of this</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>The forward legs are five days.</strong> They are the right five days &mdash; frozen specs, tape nothing had seen &mdash; but five is five. What makes them a kill rather than a bad week is that the <em>whole parameter surface</em> reversed (16/16 &rarr; 1/16, 11/12 &rarr; 0/12) and both rules were beaten by their own mirrors.</li>
<li><strong>My re-implementations are not last week's engines.</strong> Stated in full in &sect;5. The comparison is internally consistent; the absolute in-sample figures are not reproductions.</li>
<li><strong>The &ge;120pt band is n=51 with t=1.83.</strong> It passes every robustness test I can run and it is still not statistically significant. It is a shape to build toward, not a number to trust to the cent.</li>
<li><strong>The gold year-tape has no book and no spread.</strong> The $7.50 round trip is modelled, not raced, and stressed at 0.15/0.50pt per leg &mdash; the sign never changes. But it means the year tape can only refute the <em>price trigger</em>, and it does not touch the book condition the surviving gold cell actually rests on.</li>
<li><strong>The gold year-tape cannot resolve intrabar ordering.</strong> On 1-minute bars the straddle reads +$3.90/trade before the tiebreak and &minus;$7.96 after it &mdash; three times the apparent edge is one assumption a minute bar physically cannot answer. That is why it was re-raced on the 250ms quote tape, and that is the number quoted.</li>
<li><strong>Three separate look-ahead / accounting bugs were found and fixed this week</strong> &mdash; the gold straddle's one-bar offset ($86k), VACUUM's overlapping-position inflation ($9.4k of a $10.3k headline), and the chop lab's 55-second feature join. I cannot rule out a fourth. What I can say is that every conclusion here is a refutation, and leaks manufacture edges rather than destroy them &mdash; so an undiscovered leak would make this verdict <em>more</em> negative, not less.</li>
<li><strong>MGC gates were invented fresh, never ported.</strong> No MNQ gate was transferred to gold and no gold result is priced at $2.00/point.</li>
</ul>

<h3>13. Verdict</h3>

<p><strong>MOVEMENT 3 IS A NULL ON MNQ THIS WEEK, WITH ONE THIN GOLD SURVIVOR &mdash; and for the first time the null has a precise address.</strong></p>

<p>Nine new candidates reached a backtest and all nine are graves, each with a named cause of death, none of them a win-rate kill: <strong>VAC-Z1</strong> (refuted outright, &minus;$4,151 over 1,768), <strong>VAC-SNAP</strong> (strip-3 &rarr; &minus;$1,436), <strong>VAC-CUMDIV</strong> (negative at every threshold), <strong>VAC-IGN-BRK</strong> (sign-flip control earns +$3.08 against its +$3.21), <strong>BIGBOARD</strong> (strip-3 &rarr; &minus;$1,497, LOO &rarr; &minus;$1,019, arm not load-bearing), <strong>SMALLFADE</strong> (0 of 16 sweep cells positive), <strong>LOTDIV</strong> (a flat null), <strong>mgc_straddle</strong> (a look-ahead worth $86k, then dead three ways), <strong>MGC HAVEN/MIRROR</strong> (12 of 12 cells negative). And all three of last Friday's MNQ SHADOW survivors &mdash; <strong>BREAKFLOW, the pooled rider mechanism and VAC-ABS</strong> &mdash; were forward-tested on frozen specs and all three lost, each with the whole surface reversing rather than one cell.</p>

<p>What is <em>not</em> a null: <strong>the money is real and the exit converts 40.1% of it</strong> (perfect entry, mechanical exit, +$4,009 on 45 trades). <strong>Fifty of the 65 sat-out runs are boardable in the right direction a median six minutes in with 42 points still on the table.</strong> <strong>The size threshold the mandate asked for is 120 points, it is sharp, and above it boarding pays +$69.38 a trade and passes strip-3, strip-5, leave-one-day-out and long/short symmetry.</strong> And detection keeps improving as the runs get bigger, in three independent instruments, up to AUC 0.867.</p>

<p><strong>The single sentence: we can see the runs coming, we can get on board with money left, the exit does its job &mdash; and we cannot tell which way they go, so two thirds of what any detector fires on is not a run and those trades eat the whole prize.</strong> Every direction instrument this desk owns has now been ruled out at tight error bars: price (48.66%), aggressor flow (48.92%), L2 depth (49.72% &plusmn;0.67 on 21,249 book-minutes), large-lot flow (48.71%) and cross-instrument lead-lag (max &#124;corr&#124; 0.023).</p>

<div class="callout"><div class="ct">★ THE ONE STONE STILL UNTURNED FOR NEXT WEEK</div>
<p><strong>A causal SIZE predictor sharp enough to be an arm &mdash; not a direction predictor.</strong> This is a change of target and it is what this week's work earned.</p>
<p>Every hunt for three Fridays has gone looking for direction and every one has been refuted. But the &ge;120pt band does not <em>need</em> direction: the board rule supplies it causally, from the move that has already happened, and it makes +$69.38 a trade up there and passes every robustness test. <strong>The only thing missing is knowing, before the fact, that this is a 120-point move and not a 70-point one.</strong></p>
<p>Three measurements say that predictor is close but not yet sharp enough: <strong>&#124;large-lot flow&#124; AUC 0.721</strong>, <strong>ATR AUC 0.867</strong>, <strong>volume-z AUC 0.690</strong> on the 120pt+ band, all p&lt;0.0001 on real n. BIGBOARD tried the crudest possible version of this &mdash; a two-condition arm at the 2.88% most extreme minutes &mdash; and it was too blunt: it caught 7 of the top 15 but died on strip-3. <strong>The concrete next step is to stop hand-picking two thresholds and fit the arm properly against the 686-run lake-wide label</strong>, scoring it on <em>catch rate at fixed fire count</em> rather than on P&amp;L, and only then bolting the board rule on. That is a well-posed supervised problem with 686 positives, three measured features, a clean holdout in the fresh week, and an honest baseline (3.6&times; random) already established.</p>
<p>Second, cheaper, and worth running alongside: the <strong>whipsaw-block bench counterfactual</strong> from the chop lab &mdash; what benching the high-ATR / low-ER blocks would have cost in winners forgone. Everything in &sect;8 says that is where the desk's money actually goes; nothing yet proves the bench is free, and $5,414 is a gross number until someone runs it.</p>
